<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keraites</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Keraits</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Keraites were a powerful Turco-Mongol tribal confederation of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> in the 12th century, many of whom followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>. Their ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a>, known as Ong Khan, was an early patron and ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>, the future Genghis Khan, but the two fell out, and Temüjin defeated the Keraites in 1203 and absorbed them into the Mongol nation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keraites.jpg" alt="Keraites" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khiruge · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_Empire_c.1207.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Keraites first appear in history as the ruling faction of the Zubu, a large confederacy of tribes that dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> during the 11th and 12th centuries and often fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> of north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which controlled much of Mongolia at the time. Their language is disputed. Names and titles of early leaders, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a> and the title buiruk ('commander'), are Turkic, and many scholars classify them as a Turkic people. Others, among them Vasily Bartold and Boris Vladimirtsov, hold that they spoke a Mongolic language, and Tao Zongyi listed them about 1366 among the 72 Mongol peoples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Zubu broke up, the Keraites kept their dominance on the steppe. Their khanate had a central faction, which served as the khan's army and was drawn from many tribes, and an outer faction of tribes that pledged obedience but lived on their own pastures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a> reigned from the 1160s to 1203 and became the blood-brother (anda) of Yesugei; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> called him khan etseg, 'khan father'. In 1196 the Jin dynasty gave him the title Wang, hence Wang Khan. Fearing the growing power of Temüjin, the future Genghis Khan, he plotted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamukha">Jamukha</a> against him.</p><p>In 1203 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> defeated the Keraites, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a> was killed by Naiman soldiers who did not recognise him. Temüjin dispersed the survivors among the other Mongol tribes. He married Toghrul's eldest niece Ibaqa, and his son Tolui married another niece, Sorghaghtani Bekhi, who became the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Keraites were converted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> early in the 11th century. According to accounts by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> ibn Suleiman and Bar Hebraeus, the khan, lost in a snowstorm about 1007, saw a vision of Saint Sergius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and promised to become a Christian. He wrote to Abdisho, Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, and the king and 200,000 of his people were baptised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a>, who visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> in 1254–1255, found the faith mixed with shamanism and Manicheism. Some versions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prester-john">Prester John</a> legend identify that king with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a>.</p><p>Remnants of the Keraites lived along the Kara Irtysh in the late 14th century and were dispersed in the 1420s in the Mongol-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> wars fought by Uwais Khan. Afterwards they disappear as an identifiable group, though they are proposed as part of the ancestry of Kazakh, Kyrgyz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a>, Bashkir and Nogai groups.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Keraites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The steppe was then divided among rival Mongol and Turkic tribes, among them the Tatars, <b>Keraits</b>, Merkits and Naimans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Keraites</b> were a large tribal union living along the Orkhon and Tuul rivers in central Mongolia, probably of Mongolic speech with strong Turkic influences.</blockquote><a href="/state/keraite-khanate">Keraite Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
